{"name":"Dirty Blue","hex":"#3f829d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dirty","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Unclean; covered with or containing unpleasant substances such as dirt or grime.","That makes one unclean; corrupting, infecting."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["In a dirty manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dirty"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dirt","blocks":[{"text":"Dirt  is any matter considered unclean, especially when in contact with a person's clothes, skin, or possessions. In such cases, they are said to become dirty.\nCommon types of dirt include:"},{"items":["Debris: scattered pieces of waste or remains","Dust: a general powder of organic or mineral matter","Filth: foul matter such as excrement","Grime: a black, ingrained dust such as soot","Soil: the mix of clay, sand, and humus which lies on top of bedrock. The term 'soil' may be used to refer to unwanted substances or dirt that are deposited onto surfaces such as clothing."]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/King%27s_Cross%2C_London%3B_the_Great_Dust-Heap%2C_next_to_Battle_Br_Wellcome_V0013642.jpg/330px-King%27s_Cross%2C_London%3B_the_Great_Dust-Heap%2C_next_to_Battle_Br_Wellcome_V0013642.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dirt","wikidata":"Q112075129"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Dirty_Dick%27s_-_geograph.org.uk_-_821364.jpg/330px-Dirty_Dick%27s_-_geograph.org.uk_-_821364.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Dirty_Dick%27s_-_geograph.org.uk_-_821364.jpg/330px-Dirty_Dick%27s_-_geograph.org.uk_-_821364.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dirty Dicks is a Bishopsgate pub named after Dirty Dick, who once owned it and was notoriously filthy.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADirty_Dick's_-_geograph.org.uk_-_821364.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Sure_We_Can_Earthday_clean_2021_1.jpg/500px-Sure_We_Can_Earthday_clean_2021_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Sure_We_Can_Earthday_clean_2021_1.jpg/1280px-Sure_We_Can_Earthday_clean_2021_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Sure_We_Can_Earthday_clea